<section class="story-section" id="problem">
  <p class="eyebrow">01 / The problem before</p><h2>You cannot count every sentence.</h2>
  <p>Suppose your reading contains “the cat sleeps” but never “the dog sleeps.” A table of exact word sequences treats those as separate cases. More reading helps, yet possible combinations multiply much faster than the examples.</p>
  <p>Smoothing—reserving probability for unseen combinations—keeps the second sentence possible. But can learning about cats help with dogs, rather than merely leaving room for them?</p>
</section>
<section class="story-section" id="idea">
  <p class="eyebrow">02 / The big idea</p><h2>Make a prediction from a place.</h2>
  <p>In their 2003 paper, Yoshua Bengio and colleagues learned word representations and a next-word predictor together. A <strong>word vector</strong> is a short list of numbers locating a word in a learned space. Words used in similar ways can acquire nearby vectors.</p>
  <p>The model looks up vectors for a fixed window of previous words, joins them, and sends them through a <strong>neural network</strong>: layers of learned, weighted calculations. A <strong>softmax</strong> converts the resulting scores into probabilities that add to one. Training increases the probability of the next word actually seen, adjusting both the vectors and the predictor.</p>
  <p>This creates a bridge between examples. When two contexts have similar representations, the same predictor can give them similar next-word probabilities. Similarity is learned for prediction; it does not guarantee that every nearby word is interchangeable.</p>
  <figure class="idea-diagram neural-diagram">
    <svg viewBox="0 0 480 390" role="img" aria-labelledby="neural-diagram-title neural-diagram-desc">
      <title id="neural-diagram-title">Words share a vector table and a predictor</title>
      <desc id="neural-diagram-desc">The words the and cat retrieve vectors from the same table. Joined vectors enter a neural network, then become next-word probabilities. Training adjusts the table and the network together.</desc>
