</section>
<section class="story-section" id="mattered">
  <p class="eyebrow">04 / Why it mattered</p><h2>Learning could travel.</h2>
  <p>The paper showed that learning reusable word features together with a predictor could improve next-word prediction beyond the count models it tested. Neural language modeling already had earlier examples; the contribution included making this shared approach work at larger scale.</p>
  <p>A fixed window still leaves older words outside the model’s view. The next chapter asks what happens when the predictor carries a running memory instead.</p>
  <aside class="chapter-companions"><h3>Also in this chapter</h3><p>Bengio &amp; Bengio on neural joint distributions; Xu &amp; Rudnicky on neural language models; Schwenk &amp; Gauvain on bringing continuous word representations into speech recognition.</p></aside>
  <a class="next-chapter" href="/chapters/rnn-language-model/"><span>Next · 2010 · Coming soon</span><strong>Mikolov’s RNN language model ↗</strong></a>
</section>
<details class="chapter-disclosure" id="deeper">
  <summary>Go deeper <span>Architecture, learning, results &amp; limits</span></summary>
  <div class="disclosure-body">
    <h3>A fixed window, a shared lookup</h3>
    <p>Let C be the word-vector table and x the concatenation of the previous n−1 words’ vectors, with each position occupying a separate block. The same table C supplies every position. In the paper’s notation, x = [C(wₜ₋₁), C(wₜ₋₂), …, C(wₜ₋ₙ₊₁)].</p>
    <p><code>y = b + Wx + U tanh(d + Hx)</code><br/><code>P(wₜ = i | context) = exp(yᵢ) / Σⱼ exp(yⱼ)</code></p>
    <p>H maps the joined vectors into a hidden layer; tanh is a bounded, nonlinear activation. U maps that layer to vocabulary scores. b and d are biases, or learned offsets. W is an optional direct path from the word features to the scores. A feed-forward network computes an output from the current input without a recurrent state.</p>
    <h3>How the vectors learn</h3>
    <p>The objective maximizes the sum of log probabilities of observed next words, with a weight penalty. Backpropagation passes the prediction gradient through the network to the context words’ rows of C. Rows for words absent from that context do not receive that example’s input-vector update. The shared predictor still changes their future predictions.</p>
    <h3>What the experiments found</h3>
    <p>Perplexity measures how much probability a model assigns to held-out text; lower is better on the same test. In Table 1’s Brown corpus comparison, the best listed count models score 312. The selected neural configuration scores 276 alone and 252 when mixed equally with an interpolated trigram, a count model using up to two previous words. Another neural configuration scores 268 alone; the paper’s lowest validation perplexity selects the mixture with test score 252.</p>
    <p>On AP News, Table 2 reports 109 for the neural/trigram mixture and 117 for the best listed count baseline. These results concern next-word prediction, rather than conversation or factual reliability.</p>
    <h3>What remained difficult</h3>
    <p>The evaluated model keeps a fixed context window and one input vector per word, even when a word has several meanings. Computing scores across the full vocabulary is expensive. The toy removes the hidden layer, uses one context word and two outputs, and starts with invented vectors. Its learned numbers are not the paper’s measurements.</p>
  </div>
</details>
